usb: host: bcma: request the VCC GPIO non-exclusively

The Netgear R7000 powers both of its USB ports from one GPIO, so its DTS
gives the USB 2.0 and the USB 3.0 core the same vcc-gpio
(<&chipcommon 0>). bcma-hcd requests it exclusively, so whichever core
probes second fails:

  bcma_hcd bcma0:12: error -EBUSY: error obtaining VCC GPIO
  bcma_hcd bcma0:12: probe with driver bcma_hcd failed with error -16

The USB 3.0 core loses, its xHCI device is never created, and USB 3
devices fall back to the USB 2.0 controller. FreshTomato has USB 3 on
the same hardware.

Request the GPIO with GPIOD_FLAGS_BIT_NONEXCLUSIVE so both cores can
hold it. Both drive it the same way (high at probe, low at shutdown and
suspend), so sharing it changes nothing else.
--- a/drivers/usb/host/bcma-hcd.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/bcma-hcd.c
@@ -406,8 +406,15 @@
 		return -ENOMEM;
 	usb_dev->core = core;
 
+	/*
+	 * Boards such as the Netgear R7000 power both the USB 2.0 and the
+	 * USB 3.0 port from one GPIO and list it as vcc-gpio on both cores.
+	 * Request it non-exclusively, or whichever core probes second fails
+	 * with -EBUSY and its controller never comes up.
+	 */
 	usb_dev->gpio_desc = devm_gpiod_get_optional(&core->dev, "vcc",
-						     GPIOD_OUT_HIGH);
+						     GPIOD_OUT_HIGH |
+						     GPIOD_FLAGS_BIT_NONEXCLUSIVE);
 	if (IS_ERR(usb_dev->gpio_desc))
 		return dev_err_probe(&core->dev, PTR_ERR(usb_dev->gpio_desc),
 				     "error obtaining VCC GPIO");
